# REPLACE THIS. The point is the file, not the drawing.
#
# A .pix is a sprite written as text: a key mapping single characters to
# palette entry names, then a grid of those characters. Render it with
#
#   dotnet run --project src/MonoGame.GameFramework.Tools -- render-pix-all
#
# which writes the .png beside it. The PNG is what MGCB compiles; the .pix is
# the source, and check-pix-all fails the build if they disagree.
#
# Three properties you get for free and would otherwise have to police:
#
#   * It diffs. `git diff` on a .png says a sprite changed; on a .pix it says
#     which pixels.
#   * It cannot be off-palette. Every pixel names a palette entry.
#   * Alpha is binary — '.' is transparent, everything else is opaque. There is
#     no syntax for the feathered edge STYLE.md forbids.
#
# It is not a replacement for Aseprite. Hand-polish and anything much above
# 64x64 still want a real editor; this is for the small structural art — tiles,
# icons, UI frames, projectiles — where a text grid is genuinely better.
name __SAMPLE__-placeholder
size 32 32
key o outline
key E ember-2
key e ember-3-hilite
key S slate-1
key T slate-3-hilite
pixels
*2 ................................
............oooo................
..........ooEEEEoo..............
.........oEEEEEEEEo.............
........oEEEEeeEEEEo............
........oEEeeeeeeEEo............
*2 ........oEEeeooeeEEo............
........oEEeeeeeeEEo............
........oEEEEeeEEEEo............
.........oEEEEEEEEo.............
..........ooEEEEoo..............
............oooo................
.........oooooooooo.............
........oSSSSSSSSSSo............
.......oSSSSSSSSSSSSo...........
......oSSSSTTTTTTSSSSo..........
*2 ......oSSSTTTTTTTTSSSo..........
......oSSSSTTTTTTSSSSo..........
.......oSSSSSSSSSSSSo...........
........oSSSSSSSSSSo............
.........oooooooooo.............
................................
...........oooo....oooo.........
*2 ..........oSSSSo..oSSSSo........
...........oooo....oooo.........
*3 ................................
